Strategy:
    Keywords:
        Cost-Sensitive Learning pipeline.
        Prioritizing Recall -> less false negatives (critical).
        Ordinal Cost Constraint -> if uncertain, will predict higher risk than lower, (not sure if low risk or medium, probably medium).
        Ordinal Regression -> allow model to understand that High risk is higher than low but also higher than medium.
    ☐ Find equation structure.
    ☐ Experimentally determine each run. One run of valid priority weighted equation should be fine for course scope.
    ☐ Subjective mapping of low, medium and high risk attacks.
        ☐ Must explore CIA triad and CVSS.
        ☐ We have to talk about assumed Organizational Policy.

Validation & Testing:
    ☐ Must use stratified k-fold cross validation:
        1. Split your data into 5 chunks (folds), keeping the ratio of attacks equal in each.
        2. Train on folds 1-4, Test on 5.
        3. Rotate and repeat 5 times.
        4. Average the results.
        Scientific Validity: This proves your model is stable and works on any subset of the data, not just a "lucky split."
    
    For each fold:
        ☐ Train on balanced dataset, test on imbalanced dataset (90% benign). This emulates reality and the output predictions can then be used in metrics.
        ☐ Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC) -> judges if the model correctly predicts the majority AND the minority.
        ☐ Provide recall for EACH label, not for all the labels. Some attacks will be much rarer in testing dataset. Provide recalls for each label so as not to be misleading.
        ☐ Check if all attacks are predicted malicious or benign.
        ☐ Find granularity -> (did it detect all attacks or just a specific type?)

    
